<script>
import launcherSource from 'crm/src/native/launcher.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import reportsPageSource from 'crm/src/reports/index.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import UpdateNotice from 'crm/src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.abide'
import updateNoticeSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import updateNoticeTestSource from 'crm/src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/lib/Snippet.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="Ship the CRM as a native app">
        <Code code={`abide bundle
    abide bundle --out ~/Applications/CRM.app`} />

        <p><code>abide bundle [--out]</code> wraps the compiled executable in a macOS
            <code>.app</code> that opens the CRM in a window of its own. Its icon is
            <code>src/public/icon.png</code> · <code>src/public/icon.icns</code>. The first launch
            offers Start, which runs the app here, and Connect…, which opens one running at a URL.</p>

        <Snippet source={launcherSource} path="src/native/launcher.abide" />

        <p><code>src/native/launcher.abide</code> is no page of the CRM's, and what its
            <code>onLauncher</code> answers sets the window and the forms. The answer is a
            <code>LauncherOptions</code>: the window's size, floor and ceiling, how it is drawn,
            and <code>start</code> or <code>connect</code> set to <code>false</code> to drop a
            form. <code>titlebar: 'hidden'</code> or <code>'inset'</code> draws the page under
            the window's controls. The page then reads <code>--abide-titlebar-height</code> and
            <code>--abide-titlebar-inset</code> to keep clear of them, and is dragged by any
            element marked <code>data-abide-titlebar</code>. The window takes its color from the
            page's own background, and <code>dock: false</code> leaves out the Dock icon and the
            menu bar with it. Markup in the same file
            replaces the launch page, and is rendered with a <code>Launch</code>, whose
            <code>fields</code> hold one <code>ConfigField</code> per input the start form asks
            for.</p>

        <Snippet source={reportsPageSource} path="src/reports/index.abide" from={['const printReport', 'onMenu(', 'watch(']} />

        <p>The reports page adds a Reports menu while it is showing. <code>onMenu</code> is read
            the way markup is, so the menus follow the page's values. Each entry is a
            <code>MenuItem</code>: <code>'separator'</code>, an item with an <code>href</code>, one
            with a <code>MenuRole</code> the platform performs, or a <code>MenuEntry</code> made by
            <code>menuItem</code>. <code>MenuEntry.on</code> files what runs when it is picked, and
            hands back what stops it, which is what <code>watch</code> takes. In a browser nothing
            picks an item, and the page runs unchanged.</p>

        <p>With <code>menuBarExtra: true</code> the CRM also sits in the menu bar, drawn from
            <code>src/public/icon-menubar.png</code>. A click opens
            <code>src/native/menubar.abide</code> in a panel under it, a page outside every
            layout, <code>panelWidth</code> by <code>panelHeight</code>. Without that file a click
            shows and hides the window. A right click lists the page's menus and Quit, and
            closing the window keeps the CRM running up there.</p>

        <Snippet source={reportsPageSource} path="src/reports/index.abide" from={['{#if !native()}']} />

        <p>A browser has no menu bar, so there the page offers the same print as a button.
            <code>native</code> answers the version of the <code>.app</code> whose window the page
            is in, and <code>undefined</code> in a browser. The window names that version in the user
            agent of every request it makes, so the server rendering the page reads the same answer
            the page does, and the button is never drawn in the window and taken away. Any client
            can send that user agent, so it decides what a page shows, never who may do what.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Keep the native app current">
        <Snippet source={launcherSource} path="src/native/launcher.abide" from={['update:']} />

        <p>Each person runs their own copy of the CRM, so a new version has to reach every copy.
            <code>update</code> in what <code>onLauncher</code> answers is the address the copies
            look for one at: as they launch, every hour after, and when Check for Updates… is
            picked from the app menu. A newer version is downloaded beside the app and installed
            when it quits, and Check for Updates… becomes Restart to Install.</p>

        <Code code={`abide bundle --notes CHANGES.md
    abide bundle --required --notes CHANGES.md`} />

        <p>Every <code>abide bundle</code> also writes an <code>updates</code> directory beside
            the <code>.app</code>: the app zipped, and <code>latest.json</code> naming it. Upload
            the directory to the <code>update</code> address, and every copy older than it updates
            itself. A copy never installs a version older than its own, so taking an upload back
            stops new installs of it without undoing the ones that happened. Raise the version in
            <code>package.json</code> to publish the fix.</p>

        <p>The feed is signed. The first bundle makes the key, prints where it put it, and every
            bundle after reuses it. Each copy installs only what that key signed, so a host that
            serves something else installs nothing. Keep a copy of the key: a copy of the app
            trusts it and no other. To bundle on another machine, set
            <code>ABIDE_UPDATE_KEY</code> to what the file holds.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Tell people an update is waiting">
        <Example
            id="update-notice"
            app="crm"
            title="`pendingUpdate` — an update waiting for a restart"
            summary="A browser stages no update, so this card stays empty here. Its test plays the window, telling the page of an update and then of a required one."
            files={{
                'src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.abide': updateNoticeSource,
                'src/reports/lib/UpdateNotice.test.ts': updateNoticeTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <UpdateNotice />
        </Example>

        <p><code>pendingUpdate</code> holds the update the window has staged as a
            <code>StagedUpdate</code>: its <code>version</code>, its <code>notes</code>, and
            whether <code>--required</code> marked it. <code>StagedUpdate.install</code> restarts
            into it now. A required one opens a dialog that Escape cannot close, and quitting
            installs it too, so no copy goes on without it. The reports page shows the notice; an
            app with updates people must not put off renders it from its root layout, so it stands
            over every page.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
